/-! # Closed stalks of smooth integral curves

Source: Stacks Project, Curves, Tag 0C1E, `lemma-uniformizer-works`, first and last
proof sentences (blueprint: lem-a1-smooth-curve-closed-stalk-structure).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- At a closed point of a smooth integral curve the stalk has dimension one, is regular
local, Noetherian and a DVR, and its Kähler differentials over the base field are free of
rank one. Every open neighbourhood has dimension one, as does the space at the point.
The algebra structure is the composite from the field to global sections and then germs;
there is no assumption on separability of the residue field.

Source: Stacks Project, Curves, Tag 0C1E, `lemma-uniformizer-works`, first and last
proof sentences (blueprint: lem-a1-smooth-curve-closed-stalk-structure).

Proof:
1. By lem-pc-scheme-dimension-local-rings and def-pc-curve, X has topological dimension
   one. By lem-pc-integral-scheme-irreducible it is irreducible and nonempty. Apply
   lem-sc-scheme-sober to obtain its generic point eta. It differs from x, since a
   closed generic point would make X a singleton and hence zero-dimensional.
2. The strict generalization chain from x to eta gives stalk dimension at least one by
   lem-sc-scheme-stalk-dimension-generalization-chains; the upper bound follows from
   lem-pc-scheme-dimension-local-rings. Every open neighbourhood N contains eta and x,
   so the chain {x} strictly contained in N gives dimension at least one. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-open-subspace-dimension-monotone for the upper bound, and then
   def-kun-dimension for dimension at x.
3. Choose an affine neighbourhood Spec B. By
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-restrict-open-subschemes and
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-iff-smooth-affine, B is smooth over k. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-over-field-regular-local and identify the stalk with B localized
   at its point by lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization. It is regular local,
   hence Noetherian by def-weil-regular-scheme, and a DVR by lem-sc-regular-dim1-dvr.
4. The fibre over the unique point of Spec k is X, whose dimension at x is one.
   Apply lem-weil-pbc-smooth-differentials-free-rank-fibre-dimension to find an affine
   neighbourhood Spec C with differentials freely generated by e. Identify the stalk
   with C localized at its point by lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization, and
   its differentials with the localized module by
   lem-weil-pbc-kahler-differentials-localization. The linear equivalence sends c/s to
   ce/s and its inverse sends ce/s to c/s. Equality of module fractions is equality
   of coefficients in the free basis e, proving well-definedness and inverse laws.
   All maps preserve the original k-algebra structure; separability is never used. -/
theorem smooth_curve_closed_stalk_structure
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (X : Scheme.{u}) (f : X ⟶ Spec (.of k))
    [IsIntegralCurve f] [Smooth f] (x : X) (hx : IsClosed ({x} : Set X)) :
    ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk x) = 1 ∧
      (∀ N : X.Opens, x ∈ N → topologicalKrullDim N = 1) ∧
      topologicalKrullDimAt x = 1 ∧
      IsRegularLocalRing (X.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
      IsNoetherianRing (X.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
      IsDiscreteValuationRing (X.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
      Nonempty ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ≃ₗ[X.presheaf.stalk x]
        @KaehlerDifferential k (X.presheaf.stalk x) _ _
          (((Scheme.ΓSpecIso (.of k)).inv ≫ f.appTop ≫
            X.presheaf.germ ⊤ x trivial).hom.toAlgebra)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
